.TH HEE-CRED 1 "2026-09-13" "HEE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hee-cred \- real, minimal sealed credential store (GPG, or age for TPM-bound secrets)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hee cred
.B \-seal
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-recipients
.I ID[,ID...]
[\fB\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-seal
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-recipients
.I ID[,ID...]
.B \-genfrom
.I TERM
[\fB\-blocks\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-root\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-seal
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-backend age
.B \-recipients
.I RECIPIENT[,RECIPIENT...]
[\fB\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-seal
.I NAME
.B \-backend age \-passphrase
[\fB\-any\fR] [\fB\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.B <
.I KEYFILE
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-install\-keys
.B \-dir
.I PATH
[\fB\-account\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-force\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-pass
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-exec
.I COMMAND [ARGS...]
[\fB\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-identity\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-github\-app
.I ACCOUNT
[\fB\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR [\fB\-force\fR]] [\fB\-registry\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-run
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-github\-token
.I GRANT
[\fB\-registry\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-as\fR \fIVAR\fR]
.B \-exec
.I COMMAND [ARGS...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hee-cred
stores secrets as ciphertext-only files under
.BR .hee/secrets/ACCOUNT.gpg ,
or
.B .hee/secrets/ACCOUNT.age
for the age backend, and never lets a decrypted value reach an agent's own
visible output.
Retrieval is exec-only: the secret is injected into a single child
process's environment and nowhere else.
.PP
.B \-seal
(typed) reads the secret from an interactive hidden prompt. It
refuses to run at all if stdin isn't a real terminal \-\- deliberately,
so a secret can never be piped in from a chat, log, or script.
.PP
.B \-seal \-genfrom
instead pulls real words out of a local media corpus matching
.IR TERM ,
joins them into a passphrase. Still never printed \-\- flows straight
into the same GPG encrypt call as a typed secret.
.PP
.B \-seal \-backend age
encrypts with
.BR age (1)
to age recipients instead of GPG keys. An age-plugin-tpm recipient
.RI ( age1tag1... )
binds the secret to one machine's TPM: nothing but that TPM can open it.
That is how a host being installed gets secrets it can use at first boot,
before it has any GPG key.
.PP
.B \-seal \-backend age \-passphrase
seals with a passphrase instead of recipients
.RB ( "age \-p" ,
scrypt). This is the shape for a person's own identity keys: the
ciphertext is safe in a private repository, opens on any host, and is
bound to no hardware. It is the one
.B \-seal
form that reads a redirect on stdin, because a private key is a
multi\-line file, not a line anyone types; what arrives must be an
OpenSSH private key or a PGP private key block unless
.B \-any
says otherwise. age asks for the passphrase itself on the controlling
terminal, so a terminal is required even with stdin redirected. Seal key
files as they are: a key's own passphrase stays as a second layer.
.PP
.B \-install\-keys
is the first\-login half. It opens every passphrase\-sealed
.B .age
file in
.B \-dir
(age asks for the passphrase once per file), installs an OpenSSH key
under
.B ~/.ssh
(0600, directory 0700) with its
.B .pub
derived by
.BR "ssh\-keygen \-y" ,
imports a PGP key block with
.BR "gpg \-\-import" ,
creates
.B ~/.hee/secrets
(0700), and runs
.B hee\-trust anchor
when
.B ~/.hee/index/_.yaml
is missing. One status line per item, never any key material. An
existing identical key is a no\-op; a differing one is refused unless
.B \-force
is given. Exit 0 when everything is OK, 1 with a WARNING, 2 with a
CRITICAL.
.PP
.B \-pass
decrypts and sets the secret in the
.B HEE_CRED_PASS
environment variable of the
.B \-exec
child process only \-\- never argv (visible via \fBps\fR to other
users), never stdout, never a log line. The backend is chosen by which
file exists; an account with both a
.B .gpg
and an
.B .age
file is refused rather than guessed at.
.PP
.B \-github\-app
and
.B \-github\-token
give an organization's automation GitHub access without a personal token.
A personal access token of either kind is tied to the person who made it;
a GitHub App belongs to the organization. The App's private key is sealed
here like any other credential, and everything that is not secret \-\- the
organization, the App's client id, its permission ceiling, the keys its
private key is sealed to, and one grant per identity \-\- lives in a
registry (see ENVIRONMENT).
.PP
.B \-github\-app
walks one App through its life. Not registered: it prints GitHub's
registration link, pre\-filled with the ceiling, private, with no webhook,
and exits 1. With
.BR \-key ,
the private key file GitHub downloaded: it checks that the file is an RSA
key that signs, and seals it to the registry's recipients. Registered and
sealed: it opens the key, asks GitHub what the organization's installation
holds, and compares that with the ceiling \-\- more is CRITICAL, less is
WARNING.
.PP
.B \-github\-token
changes what
.B \-run
or
.B \-pass
hands the child: not the key, but one installation token for
.IR GRANT ,
in
.B GH_TOKEN
(or
.BR "\-as VAR" ).
The token names only the grant's repositories \-\- a regular expression, a
list, or the agent roster's
.I scope.repos
\-\- and a subset of the App's permissions. A grant above the ceiling is
refused before GitHub is called; a token whose granted permissions or
repositories differ from the request is revoked and refused. The child
never sees
.BR HEE_CRED_PASS ,
and the token is revoked when the child exits, so it lives as long as the
command and never longer than GitHub's one hour. The child's exit code is
the tool's.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-seal " ACCOUNT"
Seal a new secret under the given account name.
.TP
.BI \-recipients " ID[,ID...]"
Comma\-separated GPG key IDs or emails to encrypt to, or age recipients
with
.BR "\-backend age" .
At least one required. Multiple recipients means any one of them can
independently decrypt.
.TP
.BI \-backend " gpg|age"
With
.BR \-seal :
the encryption backend (default
.BR gpg ).
.B age
writes
.I ACCOUNT.age
and needs
.B age
on PATH, plus
.B age\-plugin\-tpm
for a TPM recipient. Refused if the account already has a file from the
other backend.
.TP
.B \-passphrase
With
.BR "\-seal \-backend age" :
seal with a passphrase (scrypt) instead of
.BR \-recipients ,
which is then refused. Reads a key file from a stdin redirect, or a
typed line with
.BR \-any .
Needs a terminal for age's own passphrase prompt.
.TP
.B \-any
With
.BR \-passphrase :
accept plaintext that is not an OpenSSH or PGP private key.
.TP
.B \-install\-keys
Open every passphrase\-sealed
.B .age
file in
.B \-dir
and install it (see DESCRIPTION).
.TP
.BI \-account " NAME"
With
.BR \-install\-keys :
only files named
.IR NAME\-*.age ,
installed without the prefix, so
.I spencer\-id_ed25519.age
lands as
.BR ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 .
.TP
.B \-force
With
.BR \-install\-keys :
replace an existing key that differs from the sealed one. With
.BR "\-github\-app \-key" :
replace a sealed App key (a rotation).
.TP
.BI \-run " ACCOUNT"
Like
.B \-pass
, but also exports the secret under a variable name derived from
.IR ACCOUNT :
non-alphanumerics become underscores and the whole name is upper-cased, so
.I plex\-token
arrives as
.BR PLEX_TOKEN ,
.I cf\-dns\-token
as
.BR CF_DNS_TOKEN .
This is the naming operators were already applying by hand in every
.B \-exec
wrapper, so a consumer needs no
.B sh \-c
shim to rename it.
.B HEE_CRED_PASS
is still set as well, so existing wrappers keep working.
.TP
.BI \-as " VAR"
With
.B \-run
or
.BR \-pass :
use
.I VAR
as the variable name instead of the derived one. For a tool whose expected
variable does not follow from the credential's own name.
.TP
.BI \-pass " ACCOUNT"
Decrypt the named account's secret for one \fB\-exec\fR call.
.TP
.BI \-identity " FILE"
With
.B \-pass
or
.B \-run
on an
.B .age
credential: the age identity file to decrypt with. Defaults to
.BR HEE_CRED_AGE_IDENTITY .
A TPM identity file holds a key wrapped by that TPM and is useless on any
other machine.
.TP
.BI \-github\-app " ACCOUNT"
Register, seal or check the GitHub App whose private key is sealed as
.I ACCOUNT
(see DESCRIPTION). Exit 0 OK, 1 WARNING, 2 CRITICAL.
.TP
.BI \-key " FILE"
With
.BR \-github\-app :
the private key file GitHub downloaded, sealed to the registry's
.IR seal_recipients .
An existing sealed key is replaced only with
.BR \-force :
generate the new key in GitHub, seal it, check, then delete the old key
in GitHub. Delete the downloaded file once it is sealed.
.TP
.BI \-github\-token " GRANT"
With
.B \-run
or
.B \-pass
on a GitHub App key: give the child a one\-hour installation token for
.I GRANT
instead of the key, revoked when the child exits.
.TP
.BI \-registry " FILE"
With
.B \-github\-app
or
.BR \-github\-token :
the GitHub App registry. Defaults to
.BR HEE_GITHUB_APPS ,
else
.IR ~/git/fleet\-ops/hee/registries/github\-apps.registry.v1.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-exec " COMMAND [ARGS...]"
The command to run with \fBHEE_CRED_PASS\fR set. Required with
\fB\-pass\fR.
.TP
.BI \-genfrom " TERM"
Generate a passphrase from real corpus text matching \fITERM\fR
instead of typing one.
.TP
.BI \-blocks " N"
Number of corpus\-derived words to join (default 4).
.TP
.BI \-dir " PATH"
Secrets directory (default \fB.hee/secrets\fR, relative to cwd). With
\fB\-github\-app\fR or \fB\-github\-token\fR, the \fB.hee/secrets\fR beside
the registry's repository, so both work from any directory.
.TP
.BI \-root " PATH"
Corpus root for \fB\-genfrom\fR (default \fB/var/local/hee\-corpus\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
Seal a secret, two recipients:
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-seal my\-api\-key \e
.br
    \-recipients 6A58FE47...3188,73B1462A...FDA0
.RE
.PP
Use a sealed secret without ever seeing it:
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-pass my\-api\-key \-exec bash \-c \e
.br
    'curl \-H "Authorization: Bearer $HEE_CRED_PASS" https://api.example.com'
.RE
.PP
Preferred pattern for a tool that reads its secret from stdin: seal it,
then pipe it in from the \fB\-exec\fR child, so the plaintext exists only
on that one pipe and never on a command line, in a file, or in a log:
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-pass registry\-login \-exec sh \-c \e
.br
    'printf %s "$HEE_CRED_PASS" | docker login \-u bot \-\-password\-stdin'
.RE
.PP
Seal a Wi\-Fi password to this machine's TPM, then open it on the same
machine (the identity comes from
.BR "age\-plugin\-tpm \-\-generate" ):
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-seal wifi \-backend age \e
.br
    \-recipients "$(age\-plugin\-tpm \-y identity.txt)" \-dir .hee/secrets
.br
hee cred \-run wifi \-identity identity.txt \-dir .hee/secrets \e
.br
    \-exec nmcli connection modify home wifi\-sec.psk\-flags 0 ...
.RE
.PP
Seal your own SSH and GPG keys with a passphrase for a host that is
about to be bootstrapped, then install them at its first login:
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-seal spencer\-id_ed25519 \-backend age \-passphrase \e
.br
    \-dir hosts/flippy/keys < ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
.br
gpg \-\-armor \-\-export\-secret\-keys KEYID | \e
.br
    hee cred \-seal spencer\-gpg \-backend age \-passphrase \-dir hosts/flippy/keys
.br
# on the new host, as the account, at its first login
.br
hee cred \-install\-keys \-dir ~/keys \-account spencer
.RE
.PP
Register the organization's GitHub App, seal its key, check it, and run
one command with a token that dies when the command does:
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-github\-app github\-app\-tcos\-agents
.br
hee cred \-github\-app github\-app\-tcos\-agents \e
.br
    \-key ~/Downloads/tcos\-agents.2026\-09\-13.private\-key.pem
.br
hee cred \-run github\-app\-tcos\-agents \-github\-token touchy\-claude \e
.br
    \-exec gh pr list \-\-repo Twin\-Cities\-Open\-Systems/fleet\-ops
.RE
.SH SECURITY
A secret sealed to multiple recipients can be decrypted independently
by any one of them \-\- this is intentional, not a weakness. Add every
real party that legitimately needs access as a recipient at seal
time; there is no way to add a recipient after the fact without
re\-sealing.
.PP
A TPM\-sealed secret dies with that TPM: a firmware TPM clear or a board
replacement makes it unrecoverable. Keep the plaintext's real source of
truth (a password manager) and treat the sealed copy as a delivery
vehicle, not a backup.
.PP
A passphrase\-sealed file is exactly as strong as its passphrase: keep
that in the password manager, never in a repository or a chat, and seal
key files with their own passphrases intact so a leaked sealing
passphrase still yields locked keys. Once installed, the keys rest on
the host's disk; an unencrypted root filesystem is then the weakest
link, not this tool.
.PP
A GitHub App private key does not expire. Whoever holds it can mint
tokens up to the App's ceiling until an owner deletes that key in GitHub,
so keep the ceiling as small as the grants need, delete the downloaded
file once it is sealed, and rotate by adding a new key before deleting the
old one (an App may hold up to 25).
